"""
onn_scaling_narrative.py
========================
Figures for §2.5 "Fundamental Scaling Laws", following the
three-step narrative flow:

  Step 1  fig_step1_bandwidth_scaling()
          Universal σ²_norm ∝ B law.  All architectures, all regimes,
          all parallel on a log-log plot.  Demonstrates that bandwidth
          is the single universal precision cost.

  Step 2  fig_step2_power_sweep()
          σ²_norm vs P_max at fixed B.  As power rises from μW to mW:
          background-noise dominated (slope −2) → shot-noise dominated
          (slope −1) → computation-noise limited (slope 0, saturated).
          Left panel: full sweep with regime annotations.
          Right panel: noise budget comparison at three operating points,
          for both ρ=0 and ρ=1.

  Step 3  fig_step3_averaging()
          In the computation-limited (saturated) regime, precision
          varies only with ρ and η.  Three panels:
            (a) ρ=0, N ∈ {4,16,64,256}  — averaging improves with N
            (b) ρ=1, N ∈ {4,16,64,256}  — C_coh floor, N irrelevant
            (c) N=64, ρ ∈ {0,0.25,0.5,1} — convergence at η=1
"""

